{
  "pattern_id": "4df26ba8",
  "name": "wasp_basic_aggregation_04",
  "version": "1.0",
  "created": "2026-03-19T15:56:45.762437Z",
  "solution": {
    "brief": "Sets up a two-part Wasp stochastic aggregation system where two Brep geometries are defined as discrete parts with directional connections and assembly rules, then aggregated randomly and previewed with Weaverbird Catmull-Clark subdivision smoothing.",
    "principle": "\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 05 is the counterpart to this tutorial (04) — it implements the identical two-part Brep aggregation workflow but omits the Weaverbird Catmull-Clark subdivision step. The contrast between tutorial 04 (with smoothing) and tutorial 05 (without smoothing) isolates the Weaverbird post-processing as a purely optional visualization layer. The underlying aggregation logic and part definitions are identical between the two tutorials.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 06 introduces a complementary post-processing pathway to Weaverbird smoothing (used in this tutorial 04): file export via a file update component. Where tutorial 04 extends the aggregation with visual smoothing, tutorial 06 extends it with custom part attributes and data persistence. Both are optional extensions to the core aggregation workflow.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 04 is a downstream extension of the foundational workflow introduced in tutorial 01 (wasp_01_basic_aggregation). Tutorial 01 establishes the minimal single-part aggregation pattern; tutorial 02 extends it to two parts; tutorial 04 adds Weaverbird smoothing on top of the two-part setup. Understanding tutorial 01's core 5-step workflow is a prerequisite for this tutorial.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: The wasp_04_field_driven pattern introduces scalar field-driven aggregation as a distinct advanced pathway from the Weaverbird smoothing post-processing demonstrated in this tutorial. While this tutorial (wasp_basic_aggregation_04) extends the aggregation workflow with visual smoothing, the field-driven variant extends it by biasing growth itself — using attractor or density-gradient fields to steer part placement and selection during aggregation rather than post-processing the result. Both are extensions of the same core two-part stochastic workflow.",
